<h2>Section: 2 – Case study details</h2>

<p class="q">Q.1. Describe the problem or challenge your solution seeks to address. Why is this problem important for India, and who is affected by it? (300 words limit)</p>
<p>India's carbon-compliance era has begun. Under the Carbon Credit Trading Scheme (CCTS), legally binding emission-intensity targets are in force from FY 2025-26 for ~490 industrial entities across seven sectors, with the first compliance filing due <b>31 July 2026</b> and a penalty of twice the carbon-credit price for shortfalls (ICAP, 2026). Simultaneously, the EU's CBAM entered its paid, definitive regime on 1 January 2026, exposing <b>€8–9 billion of Indian exports</b>; GTRI estimates effective price pressure of 15–22% on coal-based steel and aluminium.</p>
<p>This burden is cascading down supply chains to the businesses least equipped to bear it: India's MSMEs. Obligated corporates and CBAM-hit exporters are already demanding emissions data from suppliers. Yet the <b>8.7 crore Udyam-registered MSMEs</b> — contributing <b>31.1% of GDP</b>, <b>48.6% of exports</b> and employing <b>38.9 crore Indians</b> (Ministry of MSME, 2026) — largely cannot measure their own carbon footprint. Consultants charge tens of thousands of rupees per assessment; enterprise carbon platforms are priced for large corporates, take months to deploy, and operate in English only. A tile-unit owner in Morbi or a dyeing-unit owner in Tiruppur keeps records as paper bills and phone photos — no sustainability team, no GHG expertise.</p>
<p>The consequence is a double national loss. Economically, export-linked MSMEs risk losing orders and being excluded from formal supply chains. Environmentally, India cannot meet its NDC commitment of a 45% cut in emission intensity by 2030 while the sector producing roughly a third of GDP remains unmeasured. The affected are millions of small manufacturers, their 38.9 crore workers, and ultimately India's net-zero-2070 pathway itself. What is not measured cannot be managed — and today, most of India's industrial base cannot measure.</p>
<p><i>[Word count: 268/300]</i></p>

<p class="q">Q.2. Describe your idea (e.g., design thinking approach, community-led model, product or process innovation, etc). Additionally, how is your idea different, more practical, or more inclusive than existing solution and how does your solution support India's sustainability, resilience or net-zero ambitions? (Max 300 words)</p>
<p><b>CarbonSathi</b> is an AI-powered, vernacular-first carbon accounting and compliance copilot that makes emissions measurement as simple as photographing a bill.</p>
<p><b>How it works:</b> (1) an MSME owner uploads electricity bills, fuel invoices or purchase records — photos or PDFs, in their own language; (2) an AI document-extraction engine converts them into structured energy and material data; (3) an India-native emission-factor core (latest CEA grid factor <b>0.7117 tCO₂/MWh</b>, IPCC and India GHG Program factors) computes Scope 1, 2 and 3 emissions automatically; (4) the platform generates a live dashboard, CCTS-Form-A-style and buyer/CBAM-ready reports, AI-ranked efficiency actions with payback periods, and a carbon-credit-readiness score.</p>
<p>The idea emerged from design-thinking around how small units actually operate: paper records, WhatsApp, no experts. Instead of forcing MSMEs to become carbon accountants, the AI absorbs the complexity.</p>
<p><b>Differentiation:</b> enterprise platforms (Persefoni, Watershed) target large corporates at enterprise prices; Indian ESG tools (sentra.world, Greenio, Zoei) still assume English-speaking sustainability staff; consultants cannot scale to lakhs of units. CarbonSathi is the only combination of AI document ingestion + Indian languages + India-specific factors + CCTS/CBAM-native outputs + a few-hundred-rupees monthly price — a copilot, not a consultant.</p>
<p><b>Inclusivity by design:</b> mobile-first for low-bandwidth users, guided flows requiring zero expertise, priority onboarding for women-led MSMEs, and pricing the smallest unit can afford.</p>
<p><b>Net-zero linkage:</b> measurement is the entry gate to reduction. By making crores of businesses measurable, CarbonSathi extends India's carbon-market rails (CCTS/Indian Carbon Market) to the economy's base, directly serving the NDC 45% emission-intensity target and net-zero 2070.</p>
<p><i>[Word count: 258/300]</i></p>

<p class="q">Q.3. Explain how your solution contributes to the selected challenge theme. (Max 300 words) — How does your solution contribute to climate resilience, clean energy adoption, resource efficiency, circular economy, or sustainable infrastructure? Describe your solution and explain its environmental, social, and economic benefits, including its potential to improve resource efficiency, reduce environmental impact, enhance resilience to climate-related challenges, and support sustainable development outcomes at scale.</p>
<p>CarbonSathi builds the missing digital MRV (measurement–reporting–verification) layer for India's smallest industrial units — the foundational technology of carbon management.</p>
<p><b>Clean energy adoption:</b> the recommendation engine identifies electricity-heavy processes and quantifies payback on rooftop solar, open-access renewables and efficient motors — converting abstract "go green" advice into ranked, financeable decisions.</p>
<p><b>Resource efficiency:</b> unit-level dashboards reveal energy and material intensity per unit of production. BEE's SME programme audits in clusters like Morbi (ceramics) and Tiruppur (textiles) have consistently identified double-digit savings potential; CarbonSathi turns such one-time audits into continuous monitoring.</p>
<p><b>Climate resilience:</b> measurement shields MSMEs from this era's new shocks — CBAM tariffs, buyer exclusion, compliance penalties — protecting jobs and export revenue.</p>
<p><b>Sustainable infrastructure:</b> an open, config-driven MRV rail that regulators, banks and buyers can trust — an ONDC/UPI-style digital public infrastructure ambition for carbon data.</p>
<p><b>Environmental benefits:</b> pilot target of ~35,000 tCO₂e measured and ~2,500 tCO₂e abated annually across 200 units (conservative case), scaling to millions of tonnes as adoption grows; every measured unit becomes a candidate for reduction and carbon-credit participation.</p>
<p><b>Social benefits:</b> protects MSME employment (the sector employs 38.9 crore people) from carbon-trade disruption; trains local youth as "Carbon Mitras"; brings women-led units into green value chains first.</p>
<p><b>Economic benefits:</b> 5–15% energy-cost savings potential per unit, preserved EU order books within €8–9 billion of CBAM-exposed trade, and future access to Indian Carbon Market credit revenue and green finance.</p>
<p><b>Scale logic:</b> cluster → state → national, riding CCTS's expansion toward 740+ obligated entities whose supply chains will demand exactly this data.</p>
<p><i>[Word count: 262/300]</i></p>

<p class="q">Q.4. Mention the current development stage of your solution. Also, is it tested or validated? (300 words limit)</p>
<p>☐ Idea Stage&nbsp;&nbsp; ☐ Proof of concept&nbsp;&nbsp; <b>☒ Functional Prototype</b>&nbsp;&nbsp; ☐ MVP&nbsp;&nbsp; ☐ Simulation&nbsp;&nbsp; ☐ Pilot-Tested Solution&nbsp;&nbsp; ☐ Market-Ready</p>
<p><b>What exists today:</b> a working web platform on a production-grade stack — React frontend, Node.js/Express APIs, MongoDB, JWT/OAuth-secured accounts; an emission-factor engine seeded with the latest CEA grid factor (0.7117 tCO₂/MWh, Database v21.0) and IPCC/India GHG Program fuel factors; an LLM-based document-parsing module that converts uploaded electricity bills and fuel invoices into structured consumption data; a real-time dashboard (Socket.IO); and exportable PDF reports mirroring CCTS Form-A data fields.</p>
<p><b>Validation status:</b> factor-engine outputs cross-checked against published CEA/BEE reference values; the AI extraction pipeline tested on simulated datasets representing two cluster archetypes (ceramic kiln unit; textile dyeing unit); end-to-end flow runs from upload to generated report.</p>
<p><b>Not yet done — stated honestly:</b> no live commercial pilot yet; accredited-verifier (ACVA) integration and Indian Carbon Market portal linkage are roadmap items; vernacular coverage currently spans a subset of target languages.</p>
<p><b>Immediate next step:</b> a structured 90-day pilot with 15–20 units through a cluster association (Morbi/Tiruppur targets identified), converting simulated validation into field evidence.</p>
<p><b>Execution credibility:</b> the applicant has independently shipped two production-grade platforms — a real-time collaborative coding and interview platform with AI-driven evaluation (LLM structured outputs, multi-key failover, payments, horizontal scaling) and a fault-tolerant webhook-security platform (Redis/BullMQ, zero payload loss) — demonstrating proven capability across exactly the components CarbonSathi requires: real-time dashboards, AI pipelines and secure multi-tenant SaaS.</p>
<p><i>[Word count: 234/300]</i></p>

<p class="q">Q5. What is innovative about your solution? Describe the key innovation(s) that make your solution unique... • What makes your solution different from existing alternatives? Mention the existing alternatives used in comparison • How does it address the identified problem more effectively, efficiently, affordably, or inclusively? • What is the most novel aspect of your solution? Does your innovation reduce cost, improve efficiency or increase impact?</p>
<p><b>Existing alternatives, named:</b> (1) global enterprise platforms — Persefoni, Watershed, Sweep: powerful but enterprise-priced, months-long deployments, English-only, CCTS-blind; (2) Indian ESG software — sentra.world, Greenio, Zoei ESG: closer to home, yet built for companies with sustainability staff; (3) IIM Bangalore's free carbon tool: useful but manual-entry, no document AI, no compliance outputs; (4) consultants: credible but costly per engagement and structurally unable to serve lakhs of units continuously; (5) Excel/self-help: the actual status quo — error-prone and unverifiable.</p>
<p><b>More effective, affordable, inclusive:</b> the single biggest adoption barrier for MSMEs is data entry — owners will not transcribe bills into forms. CarbonSathi's AI ingestion removes that barrier entirely: photograph, upload, done. Affordability: a few hundred rupees monthly versus consultant engagements costing orders of magnitude more — roughly a 100× cost reduction per measured unit. Inclusivity: vernacular UI and mobile-first design reach owners no English SaaS can.</p>
<p><b>Most novel aspect:</b> an AI bill-to-carbon pipeline tuned to Indian documents (state DISCOM bill formats, informal fuel invoices, regional languages) fused with CCTS/CBAM-native compliance outputs at an MSME price point. No player occupies this quadrant.</p>
<p><b>Cost / efficiency / impact:</b> cost — ~100× cheaper than the consultant route per unit; efficiency — measurement collapses from weeks of consulting to minutes of upload; impact — carbon measurement becomes feasible for the 8.7-crore-unit segment producing ~31% of GDP that today's carbon infrastructure cannot see.</p>
<p><i>[Word count: 231/300]</i></p>

